    "# Task 2: Classification\n",
    "\n",
    "Next you will use our knowledge of classification to extract features and make predictions based on them. Here you will be using a Logistic Regression Model, keep this in mind so you know where to get help from.\n",
    "\n",
    "### TODO 1: Define the feature function\n",
    "\n",
    "This implementation will be based on ___any two___ attributes from your dataset. You will be using these two attributes to predict a third. Hint: Remember the offset!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### I will be using the popularity of a movie (number of movie ratings) and the number of genres a movie belongs to as attributes to predict whether a user will like a movie (give it a rating >= 4.0 stars) or not. \n",
    "\n",
    "#### P(liked) = B0 + B1x1 + B2x2 +e\n",
    "##### x1 = movie_num_rating\n",
    "##### x2 = num_genres\n",
    "##### y = liked\n",
    "##### B = coefficients\n",
    "##### e = error term\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Research Question: Can movie and user context predict whether a rating will be at least 4.0?\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Evaluation Statistic: Balanced Error Rate (BER)\n",
    "\n",
    "BER = 1 - (Balance Accuracy)\n",
    "\n",
    "Interpretation: A lower BER is better because it means the model is more accurately predicting whether a movie will be liked or not. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 39,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "movie_count_size = train_df.groupby(\"movieId\").size()\n",
    "\n",
    "def feature(d): \n",
    "    movie_num_rating = movie_count_size.get(d[\"movieId\"], 0) #movie popularity\n",
    "    feat = [1, movie_num_rating, d[\"num_genres\"]]  #1 is the offset (intercept) to account for bias\n",
    "    return feat #The feature function returns a list\n",
    "#NOTE: The offset is redundant here because LogisticRegression() already includes one, but I will force the intercept to be False. \n",
    "\n",
    "#Apply the feature function interatively on each row and build up feature matrix.\n",
    "train_cla = [feature(row) for _, row in train_df.iterrows()]\n",
    "valid_cla = [feature(row) for _, row in valid_df.iterrows()]\n",
    "test_cla = [feature(row) for _, row in test_df.iterrows()]"
   ]
  },
